thread:
Arjun: FYI I ran through the C# quickstart and the HttpClient example in the README is misleading — it puts the api key in the URL and uses .Result on async which will deadlock in some hosts.

Lily: ahhh, that could crash sample apps on Azure Functions. which file?

Arjun: docs/quickstart/csharp/README.md root snippet. Current snippet:```csharp
var client = new HttpClient();
var res = client.PostAsync("https://api.redwood.run/v1/generate?api_key=sk-XXX", new StringContent(json, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")).Result;
var body = res.Content.ReadAsStringAsync().Result;
```

DocBot: automated-doc-scan: security & async anti-pattern detected (severity: high). Flagged: token-in-url, blocking async calls, missing Authorization header example.

Tess: ok we should change to an async example and show IHttpClientFactory/using pattern. Also call out envvar usage and Powershell escaping gotchas for Windows users.

Jorge: agree. propose small replacement (async + header):```csharp
using var client = new HttpClient();
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REDWOOD_API_KEY"));
var response = await client.PostAsync("https://api.redwood.run/v1/generate", new StringContent(json, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json"));
var body = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
```

Lily: also add a quick note about PowerShell string interpolation: `$env:REDWOOD_API_KEY` vs bash `export`. Many folks paste `"$REDWOOD_API_KEY"` into PS and it fails.

Arjun: can we add a 1-line retry mention? e.g., backoff for 429s and avoid re-posting user actions without dedup key. Keep it short, link to retries doc.

Tess: I'll open a small PR to replace the snippet, add the envvar examples for bash and pwsh, plus a short sidenote about async best-practices and not logging tokens. I'll mention issue #4512 in the PR.

DocBot: PR created by Tess: github.com/redwood/inference/docs/pull/2099 (includes C# async example, header auth, and PowerShell envvar note).
